Local patch 0007 (qwen3.8-flash-next-exl3-tensorfold): faster EXL3 expert kernels, outputs unchanged.

Why: profiling the Qwen3.6-35B EXL3 recipe (which shares these kernels) showed the decode grouping kernel taking ~60 us a
layer at 8 streams: one block, every thread scanning every pick per expert (O(experts x picks)), ~3 ms of a 78 ms round.
What:
- cuda/exl3/experts.cu group_kernel: counts by a shared-memory histogram, the same scan numbering the used experts in id
  order, then one warp lists each expert's members in pick order (__match_any_sync, 32 picks a step). Identical lists
  (checked on random picks, 1-400 rows), 3-7x faster (32 rows: 68 -> 10 us; 128 rows: 279 -> 41 us). Its dynamic shared
  memory adds two ints an expert, so tests/cuda/test_group_kernel_smem.py's largest launch takes that into account.
- cuda/exl3/experts_prefill.cuh prefill_strip: a full pass (every member tile used, most passes) loads all A fragments
  of a K step before its multiplies, without the per-tile break; each accumulator gets the same mma in the same order:
  bit-identical, ~1-2% faster prompt experts.
Tests (GPU, Flash image): test_exl3_experts, test_exl3_prompt_experts, test_group_kernel_smem, test_qwen4_exp_exl3,
test_exl3_any_width_glm: same results before and after.

